","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<p>a</p>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<p>b</p>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<p>c</p>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<p>d</p>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p></p>\n<p><span>Answer: (C)</span></p>\n<p><span>Solution:</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"https://cms-cdn.classplus.co/parserImages/269131c5-769c-4ded-854b-804d96ebaf3c.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"https://cms-cdn.classplus.co/parserImages/1aec882d-120e-4d74-855a-bf42a2285bbe.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Test ID 60 [Discrete +TOC+ Compiler Round 2- 2026]"},{"qnum":47,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p><img src=\"https://cms-cdn.classplus.co/parserImages/65ccc909-77d1-465e-a729-2d50f77a8c3d.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<p>a</p>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<p>b</p>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<p>c</p>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<p>d</p>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"https://cms-cdn.classplus.co/parserImages/f36faf13-2feb-4999-940c-4cea667a6f60.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"https://cms-cdn.classplus.co/parserImages/578ffb79-33e3-44c5-83fb-8a96230ca4d1.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Test ID 60 [Discrete +TOC+ Compiler Round 2- 2026]"},{"qnum":48,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,D","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p><img src=\"https://cms-cdn.classplus.co/parserImages/4674e884-cac0-4fc7-952a-c5866eb50c0a.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<p>a</p>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<p>b</p>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<p>c</p>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<p>d</p>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p></p>\n<p><img src=\"https://cms-cdn.classplus.co/parserImages/6e66d127-b4ee-4d5f-a889-f905dcba014e.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" />.</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://cms-cdn.classplus.co/parserImages/a7c0c499-b367-467b-aeb6-7af8fea7939d.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"https://cms-cdn.classplus.co/parserImages/f5f066bf-56de-4793-ae90-4d74ed60d724.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p></p>","subject":"Test ID 60 [Discrete +TOC+ Compiler Round 2- 2026]"},{"qnum":49,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,B,C","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p><img src=\"https://cms-cdn.classplus.co/parserImages/ad474066-8976-40e8-a056-eaf79b64efd3.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<p>a</p>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<p>b</p>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<p>c</p>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<p>d</p>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"https://cms-cdn.classplus.co/parserImages/866bbcd5-4aae-4f01-a791-2dc833114c1e.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Test ID 60 [Discrete +TOC+ Compiler Round 2- 2026]"},{"qnum":50,"type":"MSQ","correct":"B,C","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p><img src=\"https://cms-cdn.classplus.co/parserImages/179f269c-ba83-4499-a599-5e0cd98c35cd.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<p>a</p>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<p>b</p>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<p>c</p>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<p>d</p>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><span>Answer: (b, c)<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>Solution:<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<span>FOLLOW sets (needed for SLR(1))<o:p></o:p></span>\n<p><span>For the grammar<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>E &rarr; E + T | T<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>T &rarr; T F | F<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>F &rarr; F * | a | b<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span>FIRST (E) = FIRST (T) = FIRST (F) = { a, b }.<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>Using the standard FOLLOW rules, we get:<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>FOLLOW(E) = { $, '+' }<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>FOLLOW(T) = { $, '+', a, b }<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>FOLLOW(F) = { $, '+', a, b, '*' }<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span>These FOLLOW sets are what SLR uses for reductions.<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<span>(a) &ldquo;Not SLR(1) because shift&ndash;reduce conflict on '+' when reducing F &rarr; F*.&rdquo;<o:p></o:p></span>\n<p><span><span>The item </span><code><span>[F &rarr; F* &middot;]</span></code><span> appears in some state (call it state S).<br />In that state, the ACTION entries (for all lookaheads in FOLLOW(F)) are:<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>on </span><code><span>+</span></code><span>, </span><code><span>$</span></code><span>, </span><code><span>a</span></code><span>, </span><code><span>b</span></code><span>, </span><code><span>*</span></code><span> &rarr; <strong>reduce</strong> by </span><code><span>F\n&rarr; F*</span></code><span>.<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span>There is <strong>no shift entry on '+'</strong> from that state.<br />So there is <strong>no shift&ndash;reduce conflict</strong> on '+'.<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span><span>&rArr;</span><span> <strong>(a) is FALSE.</strong><o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<span>(b) &ldquo;Grammar is SLR(1) &hellip; &lsquo;a*b+a&rsquo; is successfully accepted.&rdquo;<o:p></o:p></span>\n<p><span>From the construction above:<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>Every completed item uses its FOLLOW set for reductions.<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>No ACTION cell ever gets more than one action &rarr; <strong>no shift&ndash;reduce or reduce&ndash;reduce conflict.</strong><o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span>So the grammar is SLR (1).<br />If you simulate the parse of </span><code><span>a*b+a</span></code><span>, the SLR parser accepts it (see sketch under (c)).<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span>&rArr;</span><span> <strong>(b) is TRUE.</strong><o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<span>(c) &ldquo;While parsing 'a<em>b+a', the parser will reduce F &rarr; F</em> before shifting '+'.&rdquo;<o:p></o:p></span>\n<p><span>Trace (only the important steps):<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span><span>1.<span> </span></span><code><span>shift a</span></code><span> &rarr; reduce </span><code><span>F &rarr; a</span></code><o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span><span>2.<span> </span></span><code><span>shift *</span></code><o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span><span>3.<span> </span></span><span>In the state after </span><code><span>F *</span></code><span>, with lookahead </span><code><span>b</span></code><span>, we have item </span><code><span>F &rarr; F* &middot;</span></code><span>, so we <strong>reduce </strong></span><code><strong><span>F &rarr; F*</span></strong></code><span>.<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span>4.<span> </span></span><span>Later, after more reductions (to T then to E), we eventually **shift '+'`.<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span>So the </span><code><span>F &rarr; F*</span></code><span> reduction definitely happens <strong>before</strong> the </span><code><span>+</span></code><span> is shifted.<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span>&rArr;</span><span> <strong>(c) is TRUE.</strong><o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<span>(d) &ldquo;Not SLR(1) because reduce&ndash;reduce conflict between T &rarr; T F and F &rarr; F*.&rdquo;<o:p></o:p></span>\n<p><span>The only state containing both those productions has items:<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><code><span>T &rarr; T F &middot;</span></code><span> (dot at the end &rarr; candidate for reduction)<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><code><span>F &rarr; F &middot; *</span></code><span> (dot before </span><code><span>*</span></code><span> &rarr; candidate for <strong>shift</strong> on </span><code><span>*</span></code><span>)<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span>So in that state:<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>on </span><code><span>*</span></code><span> we <strong>shift</strong> (using </span><code><span>F &rarr; F &middot; *</span></code><span>),<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>on </span><code><span>$</span></code><span>, </span><code><span>+</span></code><span>, </span><code><span>a</span></code><span>, </span><code><span>b</span></code><span> we <strong>reduce</strong> by </span><code><span>T &rarr; T F</span></code><span>.<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span>There is <strong>no state</strong> where both </span><code><span>T &rarr; T F</span></code><span> and </span><code><span>F &rarr; F*</span></code><span> are complete (</span><code><span>&middot;</span></code><span> at the end) at the same time, hence <strong>no reduce&ndash;reduce conflict</strong>.<o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span>&rArr;</span><span> <strong>(d) is FALSE.</strong><o:p></o:p></span></span></p>\n<p><span>So the true statements about this grammar and its SLR parsing behavior are:<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span> <strong><span>(b) and (c).</span></strong></span></p>","subject":"Test ID 60 [Discrete +TOC+ Compiler Round 2- 2026]"}],"questionCount":50,"parsedAt":"2026-11-10T20:11:29.435Z"}